Hans Schwengeler wrote:

> Well, I use rdump to backup the files to a remote backup server.
> Using a bigger blocksize (i.e 64 instead the default 10(?)) decreased
> the time needed for a backup about threefold!
> 
> rdump -f venus:/dev/nrmt0h -0u -b 64 -d 62500 -s 80000 /
> rdump -f venus:/dev/nrmt0h -0u -b 64 -d 62500 -s 80000 /usr

You're over simplifying the task you're trying to accomplish here.   Think 
about how many individual places your data has to go (pass through) before it 
arrives at its destination.   Like having lots of different size pots all 
lined up, pouring water from one to the next to the next.   Your data has to 
come direct from disk (because it won't all be cached) and into the read 
buffer in the rdump process, then out into the network driver buffers where 
it's collated or broken up into suitable sizes to go over the network you're 
using.   All manner of wierd bunching and splitting could take place after 
that depending what's in the way of the path to your remote system (bridges, 
routers, etc) and then there's network contention/congestion to factor into it 
all.  Then at the remote system there's its network driver buffers to fill 
before the data gets passed on buffered up to be written out to the tape, and 
the resource usage of the system at the time.  It might be getting hammered 
because of some other task.

The chances of you getting your data to consistantly stream in a predictable 
manner over a link like that are pretty remote.

If you want tight control over the speed of your backups, you need a dedicated 
link from system to tape drive.

John




