Tru64.org now boasts Terry Shannon's follow-up article to the whole-hearted
endorsement of the Alphacide (and the party-line 'reasoning' behind it) he
presented earlier this month (IIRC that article did not even include the
opinion he expressed last summer in comp.os.vms that he believed that Alpha
development should have continued with, rather than been replaced by, the
port to Itanic).  It is interesting to note that this follow-up is
content-free, save for acknowledging that a lot of newsgroup rumbling is
going on:  in particular, it re-endorses the Compaq party line without
reservation and leaves the reader with the impression that the rumbling is
mere dissatisfaction with the decision rather than the result of substantive
questions about Compaq's purported reasoning, competence, and integrity (and
of course it therefore also makes no attempt to address any such substantive
questions, any more than Compaq has done over the 5+ months they have been
being raised).

But we now have new grist for our mill, because the white paper Terry
referred to as a primary source has now come to light (at least assuming
that it's the October paper entitled "Compaq's Move to the Intel Itanium
Processor Family" by the High Performance Servers Business Unit that I just
received from a friend but have yet to be able to find at compaq.com).  And
despite some recent suggestions by Compaq apologists that Compaq wasn't
*really* saying that Alpha couldn't keep up, as a processor, with the Itanic
sluggernaut, by George this paper says exactly that (and other things that
make equally entertaining fiction).

Its introductory abstract makes the bold statement "we believe ... that one
microprocessor, the Intel Itanium Processor Family will dominate all others
in performance by the middle of this decade" - typical Compaq market-speak
that manages to suggest it might even do so earlier (and, by any definition
of 'by' that I know of, certainly states unequivocally that it won't be any
*later*).

Even if you're a purist who asserts that the middle of this decade is 00:00
on January 1, 2006 (rather than the year-earlier date that most people would
assume and that the paper subtly suggests when it states "The reasons to go
to the Intel Itanium Processor Family in 2004-2005 are based on technology
and performance"), that's a pretty strong statement.  All prognostications I
have any acquaintance with suggest strongly that, *especially* lacking the
infusion of Alpha talent that Compaq donated to Intel *because it had
already reached this remarkable conclusion*, Itanic would have been just
about as far behind the leading microprocessors in the industry on any of
those dates as it is today.  Even in 2006 Intel would still have been
struggling to graft features like OOO execution and
SMT/'hyper-threading'/whatever you wish to call it onto a complex
architecture that was explicitly designed to avoid them - a design choice
that turns out ot have been a radically poor one for most commercial
processing.

Even if you grant Intel a full-generation lead in process technology
(something IBM and the Asian fabs might have something to say about), that's
not enough to close the current gap much, let alone allow the Itanic to
"dominate all others in performance".  In fact, even with the Alpha team's
help, it seems unlikely that Intel will be able to bring to market before
2005 an IPF member merely featuring the kinds of on-chip glue for dramatic
reductions in memory latency, increases in memory bandwidth, and improvement
of multi-processor interactions that POWER4 has today and both Alpha EV7 and
AMD's Hammer should offer next year - since their published road maps have
McKinley showing up next year at about 1.6x Merced's pathetic (integer)
performance and twice its bandwidth, Madison showing up in 2003 with just a
shrunk McKinley core and more cache, and Deerfield (I haven't seen when) as
simply a cost-reduced Madison.

Or, to put it another way, Itanic is about 2 years behind the industry
leaders in integer performance today, and appears to be 3 - 4 years behind
in being able to implement the next couple of rounds of technology advances
(first more on-chip glue for vastly improved interaction with the rest of
the system, then SMT and/or multi-core dice) that might have helped it catch
up despite that lag.  A document that suggests that this situation will
completely reverse itself in 3 or 4 years without *major* analysis to back
it up is - well, laughable is the most charitable characterization I can
come up with right now.

As one moves past the abstract, things get worse.  The 'Microprocessor
Trends' section subtly denigrates the importance of processor performance to
application performance without offering any rationale to suggest that in
equivalent environments the relationship does not remain as strong as ever,
then states that process improvements (rather than architectural advances
such as on-chip glue, SMT, etc.) have "begun to dominate performance
improvements and [are] expected to be the main source of improvements over
the next 10 years" - conveniently ignoring the fact that process
improvements tend to become available to *everyone* and that processor
designs that exploit architectural advances as well will therefore increase
their leads over those that don't.

It then offers up a pair of graphs (SPECint2K and SPECfp2K) that compare
Alpha's performance over time with other common processors, using IA32
(*not* Itanic) for the Intel performance data and then lying unabashedly by
stating that this pair of graphs "clearly shows the progress made by Intel
with the Itanium Processor Family".

Immediately following comes a graph of Itanic's predicted clock rate over
time (the time axis is unquantified but divided into sections that strongly
suggest calendar quarters).  Working backward from the expected introduction
(though perhaps not in quantity) of McKinley at 1 GHz at the end of 2002Q1,
we see 800 MHz Merced as having appeared about now (gee, I thought it was
earlier, but it does make for a more steeply-sloping performance graph) with
performance at only about 600 MHz last Spring; late next Summer we get a
McKinley bump to 1.1 GHz, then just before year's end a jump to 1.5 GHz
(which must be Madison, though this is a bit more optimistic than Intel's
current road maps that show Madison appearing in 2003 - as does, for that
matter, the illustration on page 10 of this paper as well, but what do you
want, consistency?) and continuing up to 1.8 GHz in late Summer 2003 (again,
likely a bit more optimistic than what you may hear from the company
actually doing the work).  Funny - the graph stops at that point, and AFAIK
so do Intel's ideas, and while the Alpha team will doubtless be able to help
one wonders how long it will take them to wrestle the complex problems that
Itanic's EPIC architecture presents into something noticeably better.

The most important thing to realize about that graph is that its indicated
rate of performance improvement doesn't differ markedly from what everyone
else can expect from process improvements alone, while containing only a
single *architectural* advance - the change from Merced's design to
McKinley's, which appears to be a one-time boost of something like 30% for a
given clock rate.  Which again suggests strongly that Itanic's performance
will fall increasingly behind that of competition which has more
architectural advances to offer as well.

Then begins a short paean to Intel process technology, rhapsodizing about
their consistent ability to double clock speeds every 18 months and Pointing
with Pride to the Pentium 4 as a shining example (without Viewing with Alarm
the fact that its new, high clock rates don't allow it to match the
performance of older, slower Pentium 3s in most common uses).  Again, Intel
itself is a bit more humble, having stated recently that performance
(perhaps as contrasted with clock rate) has been doubling every 18 - 24
months.  The high cost of new fabs is also implied as a reason Alpha would
have had difficulty keeping up with process advances, conveniently ignoring
the fact that Compaq has never fabbed a single Alpha (the Hudson facility
having been sold by DEC to Intel as part of the settlement for patent
infringement) but instead farmed it out to companies (Intel, IBM, Samsung)
whose business depends on keeping up with such advances.

One of the Compaq Faithful has recently asserted that Intel's vaunted new
'Terahertz' process will vault Itanic into the industry lead despite its
handicaps (and despite the presumed ability of the industry to use the same
technology soon afterward).  Intel itself says differently (watch out for
line-wrap):

http://www.silicon.com/bin/bladerunner?30REQEVENT=&REQAUTH=21046&14001REQSUB
=REQINT1=49449

Rather than using the technology to increase clock speeds by a significant
step-function, "The company said it still expects performance to double
every 18 to 24 months, but thanks to a new structure for high-speed
transistors there will be fewer problems with power consumption and heat
build-up".  Given that Itanic gulps power at 2 - 4 times the rate its
competition does to deliver the same level of performance, its concern about
heat build-up is understandable.

Following comes a sop to Alpha enthusiasts, praising its development through
EV7 but conspicuously omitting any reference to EV8 or beyond and
contributing little pro or con to the selling of Itanic that forms the
paper's main focus.  Then appears a section on I/O and memory bandwidth and
latency, emphasizing their importance but, again, conveniently omitting how
much EV7/EV8's on-chip glue contributes to their performance and the
complete lack of such glue in IPF.  In fact, in fine marketdroid form it
states "While they are affected by the microprocessor implementation, the
server design and implementation have a much higher impact on them" -
suggesting without actually saying so that all processor chips are close
enough to equal instead of acknowledging that bringing much of the glue (and
thus the server architecture) on-chip has *major* performance and throughput
advantages even if it isn't really part and parcel of the processor core.

The 'Evaluating Alternatives' section comes next, and the hands start waving
much more rapidly.  Generalizations fly about with no clear purpose save to
make the analysis appear far too complex for mere mortals to follow (i.e.,
"trust us - we're the professionals").  And when the 'core conclusions' then
appear, they are as follows:

1.  EV7, despite being essentially an EV6 core plus on-chip glue and more
cache, would be an industry-leading product in 2003 - 2004.  No disagreement
there, though POWER4 seems likely to give it a run for its money and
possibly Hammer as well.

2.  "EV8 would be competitive, but the differentiation would be less than
that realized with prior product generations."  Say, what?  EV8 had a major
new feature - SMT - not (AFAIK) on *anyone* else's 64-bit road maps - in
fact, no one else *had* road maps out that far.  The Alpha team appeared to
the the industry-acknowledged leader in this technology, suggesting that it
would at least initially be a unique advantage (Intel was also working on it
for IA32, but a more modest implementation and, as previously noted, a
technology far more difficult to meld with EPIC than it is with the OOO
approaches used by most other designs).

2 (continued). "Furthermore, this position would be predicated on nearly
flawless execution on our part."  Really?  If Itanic's success (assuming it
ever has any) had been so predicated, it would have been cancelled in 1996.
EV8 had a clear 3-year architectural lead over the most optimistic guess at
when, if ever, Itanic would accrue similar features, so one might suspect
that this allowed a good deal more latitude than 'nearly flawless execution'
would suggest.

2 (concluded).  "It revealed to us that the threat would be from Intel and
IBM."  IBM, yes, but Intel???  Itanic is 2 years behind in performance
today, and would have been even farther behind when EV8 was released:  this
isn't just wishful thinking, it's utter crap (unless one assumes that once
again they're deliberately confusing IA32 performance with Itanic's).

The remaining two 'conclusions' didn't address relative Alpha and Itanic
performance, but seem calculated to give the appearance of bolstering the
decision that so obviously had already been arrived at - a strategy that
continues in the next section ('The Complete Technology Portfolio').  The
first 'conclusion' in this section is truly illustrative of the double-speak
that characterizes so much of Compaq's spin:  it emphasizes the importance
of multi-threaded performance while completely ignoring the unique strength
that EV8 would have brought to such workloads, instead concluding that "It
does appear that we have pretty much 'played out' microprocessor-based
performance as a core part of the value realized by customers".  Just how
stupid do these hacks think their audience is?  (Or perhaps we shouldn't go
there...)  The companion conclusion is that "fewer and fewer people seem to
need bleeding edge performance", which may be true but says nothing about
the value of the improved *price/performance* that lowering the required
processor count can typically achieve for a given workload.

Then follow two more non-technical 'conclusions' that, again, do not speak
to the performance issues (nor are they particularly convincing in their own
right), and the section concludes with a reiteration of the suggestion that
the actual processor really isn't going to matter much and server design
will be king - which not only begs the same questions about the value of
on-chip implementations that I noted above but ignores the fact that Compaq
is at least currently still planning a shared-component, dual-pronged
(post-Marvel) 'Blade' server implementation that, if competently executed,
should ensure that the *only* price difference between an Alpha-populated
server and an Itanic-populated server will be the difference between the the
cost of using N Alphas in it vs. the roughly 2N Itanics it would take to
achieve comparable performance (plus the external glue they'd need to make
up for what Alpha provides on the chip).

At which point the paper ceases all attempts to present technical arguments
and descends into smarmy rationalizations about how 'industry-standard'
components (if Itanic qualifies, it must have done so without any actual
market presence) will be good for everyone - and, again, attempts to confuse
Itanic with IA32 in this regard (and in the mention of multi-threading as if
Itanic, rather than IA32, actually had such capabilities on some road map).
The authors have no shame, but I'm afraid I'm already used to that from
Compaq - just don't read it on a full stomach.  The alternative of having
continued Alpha development, fighting IBM head-to-head on performance and
leaving Itanic in the dust (where it will be anyhow, but now Compaq will be
there with it), and negotiating with Microsoft to restore Alpha to the list
of platforms supported by Windows (since Alpha should run 32-bit Windows
programs under FX!32 emulation considerably faster than Itanic will run
them - and the Win64 port to Alpha was essentially complete more than two
years ago when the project was scrubbed) was of course given no
consideration whatsoever:  if you already know where you want to go, why
confuse your passengers by offering them more attractive options?

So the paper never actually gets around to providing any support for its
opening assertion that Alpha just couldn't keep up with Itanic's
performance - and thus, in the face of the significant evidence to the
contrary, fails abysmally in its mission and leaves its authors and their
company look, if possible, even sleazier than before.  When Compaq decided
to abandon Alpha, it was abandoning a technology with every indication of
marked superiority for the foreseeable future over the processor it was
replacing it with.  And while Compaq's apologists have since attempted to
muddy the waters by contending that Itanic's volume advantage in pricing
would more than compensate for the need to use perhaps twice as many Itanics
as Alphas to achieve a given level of performance (an assertion difficult to
reconcile with a server architecture designed to support both and thus give
Alphas the benefit of the supposed higher volumes in surrounding, common
components generated by their Itanic brethren), this paper returns squarely
to the unsupported assertion that at the individual processor level Alpha
just couldn't have kept up.

Once again, Compaq appears to be relying upon the gullibility of its
customer base (and for that matter its shareholders) to save it from the
immediate consequences of its incompetence.  And it's not clear that it
won't get away with it for now - but the resulting inability to compete
effectively for at least the next 5 years will be more difficult to finesse.

- bill








