> or little-endian, like Tru64?
> The IPF hardware can do both, even at same time for different processes,
> but no practical OS will ever be bi-endian in its raw file support.

I'm not sure what you mean by the last clause above:  while applications may
(or may not) elect to embed endian-sensitive data in their files (and if
they do will certainly have problems sharing that data with other-endian
applications), that's an application-level rather than a file-system issue,
so it's not clear what application-visible implications file-system
endianness has (assuming of course that at the API appropriate conversions
are made when dealing with other-endian applications)?

>
> One camp of users or the other will be losers, in having to fix endian-
> sensitive nonportable source code before doing the "just a recompile",
> even if both sets of APIs are supported, and in not having the option
> for a binary translator for unchanged PA-RISC or Alpha binary programs.

While I agree that any migration to an other-endian environment requires at
a minimum exhaustive testing to ensure that there is no endian-sensitive
code in one's applications, I would have thought that the ability to run a
process in either-endian mode on IA64 would make binary translation
possible - and 'just a recompile' migration possible as well if an IA64
compiler that generates same-endian output code was available.  Assuming,
again, that the OS was capable of handling either-endian processes (possibly
by stub translators).

Of course, I may just be drawing a blank on some very obvious problems here.

- bill





