"David Lee" <teamosan@inet.att.co.kr> wrote in message news:9u46mi$t0s$1@news2.kornet.net...
> Does anyone know how to solve these problems?
> I have the following problems when booting the Alpha 600 computer running
> Tru-64 Unix V4.0F.
> But before I started explaining things, let me give you a brief history of
> this computer.  This Alpha used to be connected to a box through a PCI
> cable.  It needed this in order to run some signal processing application.
> Now I disconnected this Alpha computer from the digitizer without realizing
> that I need to modify some kind of configuration files so that it won't have
> to load/check the device drivers, among other things.  I don't know which
> configuration files or init.d scripts to modify?
> To make it short, during the (power on) booting process, it kept spitting
> out the following messages
>           Timeout on disconnected request
>           Active CCB at time of error
>           cam_logger:  cam_error packet
>           cam_logger: bus 0 target 3 lun 0
>           ss_perform timeout
> These messages kept spitting out every ~ 5 seconds.  I can't seemed to get
> out, tried Crtl-C, Crtl-Z, etc.  Nothing help.  I can't even find a "Stop"
> on my keyboard, so I can do a stop-A.
>
> My questions are
> 1) Which configuration or init.d script files I need to modify and How do I
> get this machine up to that stage so I can modify?  (since I can't get it
> into single user mode)
>
> 2) During the disconnecting of all the devices, the hardware engineer
> somehow change the unit number (lun) of the external tape drive.  How can
> find out which lun it was assigned before?
>
> I hope, this is not confusing and hoping someone can give me some ideas to
> solve this?
>
> I appreciate it very much
>
> Regards,

You don't need to correct the software, you need to fix the hardware.

Looks more to me like you have no termination on the scsi bus any more, or
that when the tape drive was 'renumbered' it caused a bus conflict.
Shut down the machine, check the termination, turn off the tape drive.
Boot again.
look through the binary error log for 'old' boot logs and you should find
the ID of the tape drive prior to the mistake.
Shut down again, set the lun on the tape to the correct value, then
power on the tape drive, power on the computer.

HTH
Adam Price
PS
stop-a is a 'sunism' which doesn't apply on Alpha Hardware.
The equivalent is the 'halt' switch on the front of the machine although
some workstations don't have one.




