didn't offer the combinations when I used it last 3 weeks ago...

Finally, the Ultrium drive is only available in the "Surestore" HP
packaging, which is primarily targeted at their PC & non-HP server
customers.  The preferred storage line for the "Enterprise" product line is
the SMART Storage system.  Look at the DLT8000 drive page for an example of
what I'm talking about:

(http://www.products.storage.hp.com/eprise/main/storage/DisplayPages/summary.htm?DataPage=dlt8000)

"    * HP SMART Storage  products are optimized for customers with UNIX
computing environments, including HP-UX and Solaris, and who purchase
products directly from HP."

SMART storage is all your average HP9000/3000 sales rep will push at you,
from what my experience has been.  And it should be noted that my employer
(EDS PLM Solutions, formerly Unigraphics Solutions) has been one of HP's
larger VARs for years, so I would feel confident that our customer base is
respective of the larger HP9000 base.  That said, HP will sell you whatever
you're willing to buy, and if Ultrium is what the customer wants, they'll
get you Ultrium.  It just takes the dumb sales rep another 2 weeks to figure
out how to order a new server from the factory that way (personal experience
here, on Surestore vs. SMART storage product lines).

: And go to
: http://www.products.storage.hp.com/eprise/main/storage/tapebackup/selectionPages/tapeTechComp.htm
: then tell me that HP aren't favouring Ultrium over DLT

No, it looks to me like a simple page showing the plusses of the various
formats they offer.  For example, they have some glowing things to say even
about 9840, a format that doesn't see a whole lot of use outside of IBM
environments (last I checked).  On the other hand, the specs for Ultrium
look better than before...

: Plus I can find no reference to SuperDLT products on HP's web site
: under tape storage.

Last word I heard on this one was that Quantum (or whomever owns the
mechanism rights now) was charging an arm & a leg for SuperDLT.  Last I
checked, none of the major system vendors were offering OEM-versions of the
SuperDLT drives yet, and I believe it was for just such a reason.  If I can
find some reference to this for you I'll post it later, but that was the
word through the grapevine.

: Finally Ultrium has only been around a few months - not 2-3 years.

Ultrium is one of 2 implementations of LTO technology, or "Linear
Tape-Open".  LTO was/is a consortium of IBM, Seagate, and HP to build new
tape formats based on a common set of open standards.  As you'll see from
the link below, Ultrium & Accelis (a dual-reel LTO format) licenses have
been available to manufacturers since April of 1998!  I've been personally
following the LTO formats since about September of 1998.  

http://www.ltotechnology.com/license.html

Licensing participants include:

http://www.ltotechnology.com/licensing/lic_parts.html

Ultrium is a DLT-style implementation, as favored by HP & Seagate, whereas
Accelis is a nod to IBM's Magstar line, where the tape format is dual-reel
and the "home" position of the tape is in the middle of the reel (i.e. with
both reels containing half of the overall tape).

HP has only this year finally introduced Ultrium drives, so that may tell 
you something about the hurdles the format has faced.  I'm not sure when the
first drives were introduced or who made them, but I seem to recall that
Ultrium drives were available since at least fall of 2000.

However, I did note that when I reviewed the Ultrium drives @ HP's web site
just now, they are offering drives specifically for HP 9000 servers.  
Somebody should clue in their sales reps on that fact!  The problem is that 
HP's "Enterprise" sales reps (9000 series HP-UX boxen) have had the SMART 
storage product line all to themselves - it's built using HP components by 
their division, thus they see the profits on their bottom line.  The Surestore 
stuff used to be a seperate division (might not anymore), hence when they sold 
Surestore stuff they were passing profit out of their bottom line to the other
division, which didn't really help them directly enough...

: In summary I cannot make sense of your experience or post at all.

Hopefully this helps comfort your sense.  I've been working with enterprise
server installations for about 7 years now, which is a short time compared
with many here but quite some time nonetheless.  Hopefully I've cleared
things up and given you some reading for more research.  Personally, Ultrium
looks like an interesting and potentially promising format.  However, I've
always been a big fan of DLT (I've got an older DLT-IIIXT drive here at home
for my own personal backups), and if SuperDLT can be competitive with
Ultrium but provide some level of backward compatibility, then all the
better.

Chris

