If we have a NIS master server with no slaves, then is it true we don't
really need yppush (or a yppush line in the NIS Makefile) if there are no
slaves to push the maps out to?

We have a DU 4.0E server which is the NIS master for a NIS domain called
gfd.ma, with the map source files in /usr/var/yp/src as normal. Everything
works fine and there is a ypservers map which lists the master server only,
so yppush executes without problems whenever the maps are remade.

Not long ago, another NIS domain, called applied.ma, was added to this
server using a different set of maps in /usr/var/yp/applied.src and a
different Makefile is run every night to update these. The ypservers map
was copied across from the gdf.ma domain. But this Makefile invokes yppush
with hangs with this set of maps - the command:

	yppush -v -d applied.ma passwd.byname

causes yppush to just sit there and say nothing. Ctrl C will kill it and
the clients still pick up the updated maps. As yppush is invoked from the
Makefile, both jobs stall.

I've no idea why this is happening but rather than spend time finding
out what's wrong, if we don't need yppush (as we have no slaves) then I
could just comment the yppush lines out of the Makefile to allow it to run
to completion?

Andy

