M. Ranjit Mathews wrote:
>
> Many notebook computers are already made in Asia with only the label
> being slapped on by a US company. Consider Qanta computer in Taiwan.
> It puts together notebook computers by the truckload for US
> "manufacturers" who are really only reselling (rather than
> manufacturing) them. It's just as easy for a US company to outsource
> manufacturing to Asia as it is for an Asian company, so why would US
> companies not be able to compete?

The only problem with this model is what happens in low demand.  An 
outsourced deal usually comes with some kind of estimate about how many 
units are going to be sold by the vendor.  In order to be as cost 
competative as possible, or even profitable, the outsource manafacturer 
will tune their manufacturing process to meet that need, and the vendor 
usually agrees to purchase a fixed quantity for sale.  If they suddenly 
can't sell that number they are in trouble because they are still obligated 
to buy the products from the manufacturer.  Product which then sit on their 
shelves as surplus inventory waiting to be sold.  This can kill a vendors 
profitability.

Outsourcing 'Just In Time' manufacturing can be very profitable when the 
going is good, and very dangerous when times are hard.



