In article <MvmB7.12053$Fx6.4296649@typhoon.columbus.rr.com>,
	"hlee5" <hlee5@columbus.rr.com> writes:

> I was trying to install tru64 5.1A to my workstation(21164 533, 2m Cache,
> pc164lx, srm console, console version 5.71, scsi 4.3, 12X scsi Cd rom, MGA
> card, intel lan card, sound blaster 64awe, lsi logic Scsi card.

> When I installed tru64 from CD, I saw some error message which is that tru64
> couldn't assign disk lable , so
> the installing process was stopped?

I'm willing to bet that the hard disk that you are installing onto
once had an operating system on it.  I'm even willing to bet that the
old operating system was linux.

Here are two suggestions:

1.  If you have another SCSI system (say, an x86 with a SCSI BIOS on
    it), see if you can do a low-level format of the disk.  I don't
    think there's a low-level SCSI disk formatter in the SRM console,
    but the BIOS of most PC SCSI boards usually have this function.

2.  Another solution requires a SCSI disk from a system running Tru64
    Unix already.  Install the "bad" disk onto that system, and boot
    to the SRM console.  Use the "cat" command to copy the "good" SCSI
    disk to the "bad" one.  In the first megabyte or so is the
    partition table, which is unreadable on the "bad" disk.  The copy
    will copy a "good" partition table (albeit one that may not apply
    to the disk), which can then be relabeled during the install.

I do a lot of work with Linux and Tru64 Unix systems, and I have found
that one of these two solutions usually works.

