Gary Hansford wrote:
>
>> John McNulty <knode@jmtl.com> wrote in message
>>
>> Btw, what do you mean in 4 & 7 by "Hardware Replication"?  I'm not
>> familiar with that term.
>  
>   In the Original question, the guy was running LSM to mirror between
> two different RAID cabinets (probably miles away from each other).

Why probably?   LSM is normally used locally on pre-V5 systems because it's 
the only way of mirroring that gives you the benefits of HBA redundancy and 
increased I/O bandwidth across HBA's.  Of cause the negatives are that is 
adds considerable overhead to system management and disaster recovery and 
is also CPU bound on the host system.

>You
> can get software which allows the HSG80's to mirror disks in one
> cabinet to another HSG80 cabinet miles away (Hardware Mirroring).

Oh I just clicked .. you're talking about DRM ( Data Replication Manager ). 
You're getting confused, this is not Hardware Mirroring at all.  It's 
replication, which is something very different.  Its function in life is to 
provide an offsite copy of your data, up to 100km away, as up to date as 
possible, for either disaster recovery or data migration.   Mirroring 
behaves very differently .. for example if I issue a read operation against 
a mirror, the data can be returned to me from either side of the mirror 
(which ever one gets it first).  But with DRM this doesn't happen.  

Now I've not implemented a DRM solution, or even seen one, but I'm pretty 
sure that FC Zoning is used to hide the remote HSG's and disks from the 
'live' hosts systems, while enabling visibility for the 'live' HSG's so 
that they can replicate the data.   So there's no way this could be 
considered to be a mirror.

> Although this has been around for a while, Tru64 v4.0F does not
> support dual-path or auto-failover in the event of the first HSG80
> cabinet failing. Thats why LSM has been used to perform this role.

It's got nothing to do with dual failover, DRM is an inter-HSG operation 
and the hosts systems are not involved.
 
>   What I was getting at... with the use of v5.1 you should now be able
> to use the HSG80 software (its an upgrade option) and let the
> controllers do the mirroring and remove the Tru64 LSM mirroring. How
> should you more from the v4.0f mechanism of LSM mirroring to HSG80
> based Mirroring system and has anyone done such a thing?

No.  You can get rid of LSM when you go to V5 because if the topology 
(connectivity) of the SAN permits it, more than one HBA on one system will 
be able to see a RAID device on an HSG pair.   In fact, if the HBA's are in 
the same RAD (for Wildfires this is a QBB) then the system can interleave 
I/O across them.

>   Thanks for the feedback so far, its getting to be an interesting
> topic...

No problem.  Glad to help.

John



