He'll need to do a little more than that.   The system crashed in the first 
place because the system disk name is no longer the same as it was on the 
original system, so it's looking in the wrong place for swap.  

After booting to single user mode he'll need to 'mount -u /' to remount the 
root filesystem Read-Write before he can change any files.

If the root, /usr (and potenitally /var) filesystems are UFS, then he'll 
need to change the device names in /etc/fstab.   If they are ADVFS, then 
he'll need to remake the links in the /etc/fdmns/{root,usr}_domain 
directories to point to the right devices.    Then he can mount the other 
filesystems.

Also, if the system is configured for eager swap, then the 
/sbin/swapdefault soft link will need to be repointed to the primary swap 
device.

Then there's the system's name, IP addresses and aliases to think about.  
Changes to /etc/hosts and /etc/rc.config may be required - especially if 
the system model is different and the ethernet device is not the same type.

There are potentially other things to look our for too.  Sendmail config, 
DNS config,  etc.

Sometimes it's faster and simpler just to do a new install :-)

John


