>> You may be able to get better performance using AdvFS striping (man
>> stripe).
>
>This seems to be striping on the file level?

AdvFS (still as far as I know) supports a command called "stripe"
When run on an empty file it tell AdvFS to evenly distribute the
file's data across some specified members of the domain the file
is on.  Left to itself, AdvFS will tend to fill up a volume with
data and then go to the next volume.  Separate files may be put
on different device, but a single file tends to fill up a device.

One could call the default striping, if one wanted to argue that
"device size" is a reasonable striping chunk size...

>
>I think I will look into that.  I don't know if our campus license
>includes this PAK.  What is the product name, the license file name?

I think the license name is LSM-OA.  Nothing particular needs to be
installed if you already have the LSM (Logical Storage Manager subsets
installed).  It does license use of the LSM GUI as well as mirroring
and striping (and other).  The base system can still concatentation
and slicing off volumes from a larger space without the license.

