I'm an ex Digital/Compaq employee of 13 years, with 11 in the UK Unix 
Support Group, now 2 years freelance in the Tru64 space, and I'm praying 
that the merger collapses.

It doesn't do me any good at all, or my brother for that matter, who works 
for a large UK reseller specialising in Compaq systems.  When I asked his 
opinion, his response was "HP have their own chain of resellers, so where 
does that leave us?".  

I see no benefit to this for customers, employees or shareholders.  The 
product overlap is so massive that grafting the two companies into one will 
be the equivelant of performing a major multiple organ transplant, with 
most of the duplicate parts thrown out.

I have no confidence that the surgeons actually know what they're doing, 
and even if the patient does survive without bleeding to death first, what 
kind of Frankenstein creation will be left afterwards?

The truth is that customers don't want this.  I've not heard one single 
customer comment (that wasn't paid for) stating this is a good idea. The 
employees I've spoken to (also share holders) don't want it and have 
unanimously said they will vote against it.  By all accounts the rest of 
the shareholders are non too happy either, after watching their investments 
get butchered on the stock market after the announcement.

The only people that do want it are Carley and Capellas, who seem to be off 
in some cloud cuckoo land of their own. 

The GS (Wildfire) platform was a huge success for Compaq and the prospects 
for Tru64's continued growth had never looked brighter; what with a very 
fast and well engineered product supporting it, and the seeds of TruCluster 
development finally maturing and bearing fruit.  I am stunned that Capellas 
could not see or did not believe in the future revenue potential of Tru64 
and VMS on Alpha & IA64.  

Actually, I just want to comment on that last point.  I do think that the 
decision to move from Alpha to IA64 was a good one, and even if the deal 
pancakes this should still go ahead.  Intel may not be able to out design 
Alpha on their own, but advances in their fabrication technology have more 
than made up for it.  

The GS & ES range of systems are a fantastic technology in their own right, 
and will still be world beaters with EV7 Alpha & IA64 chips inside.


John McNulty


