"Axel K�ster" wrote:
> 
> Hello,
> 
> I have  a DS20 with True64 4.0G. When booting the system I find the tape at
> mkd400. When coming up to standalone, I want to make the special devices for the
> tape. As I remeber, the formula was BUS-IDx8+SCSI-ID of the device. But this
> failed. During bootup I found a message regarding a tz44, which workes fine.
> 
> But: What is the numbering scheme behind this????
> 
> Thanks for help,
> 
> Axel

mkd400 is a SRM console level name, meaning a Magnetic ("MK") tape
device on the 4th SCSI bus ("D") at SCSI ID 4 (4*100) on that bus. These
naming conventions are described in the owners manual, or
firmware/console manual, depending on your box.

If the kernel recognized bus'es in exactly the same way as the SRM
console, you would thus end up with a tz36 tape device. But
it generally doesn't. (There is probably a summary somewhere on the
alpha managers mailing list (search available at http://www.tru64.org)
with some discussion from either Tom Blinn, or Alan Rollow about this.)

Personally, I try to make sure the kernel gets configured the way I
think about the devices/bus order, so that I can do things like keep
configurations similar on different machines.

