>But how do i access the tape?

It depends on the feature set you want.  Most utilities that work
with tapes default to /dev/rmt0h, so for most things you don't have
to specify any name; just use whatever commands work with tapes.

If you want more detailed control, then you have some options.  The
basic form of a tape name is:

[n]rmt#{lmah}

where:

[n] - The prefix indicating that this is the no-rewind on close.  Generally,
      when a program closes the tape drive, the drive rewind the media.
      Using one of these names, allows you to read and write multiple
      tape files.

rmt - The general name for a tape device.

#   - The tape unit number.  These are assigned sequentially as tape
      special files are created; rmt0, rmt1, rmt2, etc.

{lmah} - This is the density code.  'h' is the highest density, which
         for most tape drives is their highest native density with
         hardware compression turned on.  The tz and mtio manual pages
         will describe the remaining densities.  One is the highest
         native density without compression (probably 'l').  The others
	 depend on the tape drive.  In the case of DAT drives, I think
	 density follows the media.  If this is the case, then only
	 two of the letters matter; h and whatever is used for non-
	 compression.

