I can't really give you any guarantees without taking a closer look at your 
specific configuration.  Steps 1-3 sound ok, but bear in mind that in 
moving from V4.0F to V5.1a you are changing from seperate, local system 
disks to Cluster Common disk(s) that need to be in common storage.  Make 
sure you have a good read of the steps required for doing this on a 
Fibrechannel SAN, in the Cluster Hardware installation guide.  Also make 
sure your HSG's, KGPSA firmware and system console firmare are at the right 
rev levels before you start. 

Btw, what do you mean in 4 & 7 by "Hardware Replication"?  I'm not familiar 
with that term.


Bear in mind that when you move to v5.* the device files are no longer 
mapped to the devices according to their physical location, they're mapped 
to the device's unique WWID (World Wide ID).  In the case of HSG's the HSG 
invents and attaches a new WWID to a storage container (mirror or stripe) 
when it's initialized. It has nothing to do with the value of the LUN 
(unit) although the system won't see it until the storage container is 
assigned to one.  

So once you've backed everything up, removed LSM, re-connected your 
switches, changed your HSG's to multibus failover, reorganised your disk 
farm into whatever you want and created your new storage units, be aware 
that your device names will have changed!


Hint: Here's something that will make your life easier.  When you're 
creating your Units on the HSG, give each Unit an ID number (using the set 
command).  Make each number relevant to the HSG pair and unit number value. 
For example, if you have an HSG pair you've called HSG05a & HSG05b and 
you're creating a Unit called D5, give D5 an ID of 503.  

Later, when you list your devices with 'hwmgr -view dev' you'll find that 
the bus-target-lun mapping on the right hand column is replaced with the ID 
numbers that you've assigned.  So given any unix device file name, you will 
instantly know where it's physically located and which Unit to go look at 
from a hwmgr listing.  


Another hint:  DON'T use unit D0 .. In Transparent failover the HSG's 
manage device failover.  With Multibus failover this is managed by the host 
systems and V5 needs LUN 0 free to communicate with the HSG's.  Also as 
part of this, make sure the HSG's are set to use SCSI-3 not SCSI-2. 


>  Or is there a better way ??

Not really.  Depending on the size of your storage it's quite a big change. 
If you're time constrained then you could phase it in over a number of 
different days. 

Thinking about it, after upgrading the OS you could probably do step 5 next 
(rewire and multibus) without upsetting things, because the device mapping 
won't change unless the storage container gets destroyed.  Potentially the 
most time consuming part (in terms of downtime) will be reorganising your 
disk layout and restoring from backup.  

If this is a critical box then you should run through the procedure you 
intend to follow on a test rig first to give you some confidence. 



