No, I don't believe so.

If EV7 is almost complete, then the remaining money to be spent will be fairly
minimal, especially if PCs lose more money than expected.

Consider that Alpha/VMS is profitable due to its installed base. Of course
Compaq and HP have factored in that they will no longer get new customers to
adopt a dying platform. But they have existing customers who are very
profitable due to their support contracts and prospects for upgrades.

Furthermore, if you scuttle Alpha now, it means that the expertise that built
the wildfire boxes and who will adapt those boxes to the new interconnect for
EV7 will go away. It is important for HP to keep those folks inside of HP so
that when IA64 becomes palatable, they can design a IA64-based Wildfire style machine.

The way I see it, Alpha and VMS are put on life support to stay in a coma
until IA64 is mature enough, at which point they can do transplants of all the
cool stuff. They have already announced that they will transplant some of the
cool Tru64 stuff to HP-UX.


