Why?  People keep saying (e.g., when VMS default file-access performance is
compared with Unix's, or when questions about future IPF performance are
raised) that performance isn't at the center of why people purchase VMS.
And limiting Alpha to current ES45 and Wildfire systems is just about the
*only* way that migration to, say, Madison can be made to resemble an
'upgrade' (which should be an issue in the port time-frame:  unless Intel
started working on on-chip MP and memory support quite a while before they
acquired the Alpha team, 2005 is the earliest one could hope to see it in
production).

So since at some point there'll inevitably be a flat period in the
performance rise of systems supporting VMS (while IPF catches up with a
stagnant Alpha), letting it occur now rather than 3 years from now isn't an
obvious problem (and reduces the interval over which people will keep seeing
IPF as a performance-inferior alternative).

>
> Whether Compaq's management is interested in VMS or not is perhaps less
> important than the contractual obligations Compaq has WRT the OS. Can
> management fiat trump DII-COE or other incumbent agreements?

Not an issue, unless those agreements specify some kind of obligation to
continue following technology curves rather than just a continuation of
support for current technology.

- bill





