Or you can just link you templates staticly into every module that uses
them. This is what GCC does. There are two problems with doing this. 1) This
can cause you executable to become large. 2) Static data that should have
only a single copy will be duplicated.

If gcc is your compiler of choice, you can do "-std gnu" to get Compaq C++
to act more like it. If you prefer a more ANSI/ISO compliant compiler, but
want templates compiled staticly, you can do "-tlocal".





