Hi Chad,

Careers are made on the answers.  Are you going for performance, or for
reliability, or for both?  Lab system or production?  Amount of tolerable
down time per unit time?  Do you understand the performance profile of
the older database, specifically WRT tablespace bandwidth and redo-log
and rollback-segment activity?  You MUST understand all this before you
even think about what you're going to use as the new design.  There is no
"one config fits all" answer for your new system.

Let me add some confusion: you have a third option for drive grouping and
that's the hardware-RAID capabilities of the RAID controller.

For a basic setup with little or no consideration given to performance,
which appears to be the case with the current system (no offense intended
but after all there is only one mount point for the database itself), you
could make a couple of hardware-RAID5 sets out of the RAID disks, and
thus Tru64 would see only 2 disks.  It would give you some level of fault
resilience at the disk-controller level and would preclude your having to
learn LSM (extremely capable but there is a bit of a learning curve).
AdvFS for disk grouping would be my last choice not because of any
inherent problems with it but rather because I've never used it for that
purpose.  I'm a huge fan of LSM and so that's what I've used for years.

Anyway, if you post your phone number here I'll drop you a line and help
you through this.

--

