Which, plus a buck or so, will buy you a cup of coffee.

We don't seem to have seen any cash influx from Intel for the deal in the Q3
results, which suggests that any compensation may have been in the form of
discounts.  But we don't see any profit from Intel-based products in Q3
either, so what does that leave us with?

There's a very real question whether Intel has anything of significant value
to offer Compaq.  It apparently can't compete with AMD on IA32 pricing, so
unless AMD can be deprecated or destroyed there's really nothing it can
offer Compaq in the low end short of selling IA32 processors at a large loss
(anything less wouldn't noticeably affect total system cost) - just to make
them cost-competitive with anyone else's AMD systems.  And the value of IA64
has yet to be established:  it certainly doesn't promise to *do* anything as
well as, let alone as cost-effectively as, its competition - ever, and its
inability to run IA32 code at competitive speeds makes any claim that it
will replace IA32 laughable.

I suppose you could say that *now that Compaq has wholly committed to IA64
as a platform* Intel can do it some favors, but given that it abandoned a
far better platform (including one that has the demonstrated ability to run
IA32 code far faster than Merced can...) to obtain those favors the
reasoning behind the decision remains obscure.

- bill





